---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';

const base = import.meta.env.BASE_URL.replace(/\/$/, '');
const updated = '2026-06-16';
---

<Base
  title="Launching Zone 8a Garden: One Bed, Three Crops, All Winter"
  description="An executive summary of zone8a.com: a documented high-rotation raised bed that carries a Zone 8a garden from spring greens into a winter brassica harvest."
>
  <p><a href={`${base}/`}>&larr; Home</a></p>
  <h1>Launching Zone 8a Garden</h1>
  <p><em>Published {updated}</em></p>

  <p>
    <strong>Zone 8a Garden</strong> is now live at <a href="https://zone8a.com">zone8a.com</a>.
    It documents one thing in full: a single 4x8 foot raised bed run as a relay that
    produces three crops in one year and keeps cutting greens straight through winter.
    No sprawling almanac, no hundred half-finished tips. One bed, one sequence,
    start to finish, for USDA Zone 8a.
  </p>

  <h2>The idea in one paragraph</h2>
  <p>
    Zone 8a gives you a long shoulder season: the first hard frost holds off until
    roughly November 10. That is enough runway to grow three crops in the same bed
    if you treat it as a relay instead of a single planting. Spring spinach goes in
    early and comes out by late spring. Summer bush beans follow, fixing nitrogen
    to rebuild what the spinach drew down. Then fall and winter brassicas, broccoli,
    cabbage, cauliflower, kale, and collards, mature into the cold, when their flavor
    actually improves. Each handoff is deliberate. The heavy-feeding brassicas inherit
    a bed that is ready for them rather than one that needs rescuing.
  </p>

  <h2>What the site covers</h2>
  <p>
    The work is split into six guides across three stages, Plan, Plant, and Protect:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>
      A <a href={`${base}/guides/planting-calendar/`}>master planting calendar</a> of
      eleven crops with varieties, seed-start and sow dates, days to maturity, and
      harvest windows, all keyed to a November 10 frost date.
    </li>
    <li>
      A <a href={`${base}/guides/bed-layout/`}>polyculture bed layout</a> that stacks
      crops in three tiers by height and light, so a 4x8 bed does the work of a larger one.
    </li>
    <li>
      The <a href={`${base}/guides/succession-sequence/`}>succession and soil method</a>,
      including the chop-and-drop handoff and the shallow manure amendment that move the
      bed cleanly from one crop to the next.
    </li>
    <li>
      An <a href={`${base}/guides/pest-management/`}>integrated pest management plan</a> for
      the August transplant window, when brassica pests peak: row covers, a Bt spray schedule,
      trap crops, and a homemade insecticidal soap recipe.
    </li>
    <li>
      The <a href={`${base}/guides/companion-planting/`}>companion planting rules</a> that hold
      the layout together, including the one isolation rule you cannot skip: keep fennel out
      of the bed entirely.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Why it is worth trusting</h2>
  <p>
    The planting calendar and bed layout are ported from a verified working plan. The pest
    and soil guidance is written to be checkable: the insecticidal soap concentrations, the
    warnings against dish-soap and home-brew sprays, and the caterpillar controls all cite
    university extension and IPM sources directly, from Colorado State, UC IPM, Maryland,
    Minnesota, Clemson, and Penn State. Where a procedure was reconstructed from standard
    Zone 8a horticulture rather than a single authority, the site says so and tells you to
    confirm product labels and local extension guidance before you spray. Nothing reconstructed
    is dressed up as gospel.
  </p>

  <h2>Who it is for</h2>
  <p>
    Anyone in Zone 8a who wants a complete, opinionated rotation they can copy this season,
    and anyone in a nearby zone who can shift the dates to their own frost calendar and keep
    the relative spacing. The dates are the only part tied to this microclimate. Everything
    else travels.
  </p>

  <h2>Take a look</h2>
  <p>
    Start with the <a href={`${base}/guides/overview/`}>overview</a>, then walk the calendar,
    the layout, and the pest plan in order. If you run a raised bed in a cold-winter-but-mild
    climate, the relay is built to be lifted whole.
  </p>

  <p>
    Built and maintained by <a href="https://blanewarrene.com">Blane Warrene</a>.
    The site is a static build; if you are curious about how it is put together,
    there is a link in the footer.
  </p>
</Base>
